1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,250
What does it take to build a bank's e-commerce acquiring system from scratch?

2
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:10,464
In this series we build one, part by part, and this map is where we track every step.

3
00:00:10,464 --> 00:00:18,940
On top are the three users: the merchant's server, the buyer, and the bank admin. Inside is one modular monolith with six modules.

4
00:00:18,940 --> 00:00:24,726
Partner owns the merchants: who they are, their company documents, and whether they may sell.

5
00:00:24,726 --> 00:00:27,869
Commission decides the fee on every payment.

6
00:00:27,869 --> 00:00:34,047
Terminal knows which bank terminal, the MID and TID, each payment goes through.

7
00:00:34,047 --> 00:00:39,404
The BIN module knows which scheme and which bank issued a card, from its first digits.

8
00:00:39,404 --> 00:00:43,726
The card vault keeps card numbers encrypted, and hands out tokens instead.

9
00:00:43,726 --> 00:00:48,095
Payment creates every payment, debits the card, and resolves the outcome.

10
00:00:48,095 --> 00:00:57,738
Below the app, separate services do one job each: email, a file service for documents, and an adapter for each card scheme, Uzcard and Humo.

11
00:00:57,738 --> 00:01:08,119
The shared infrastructure is Postgres, Redis, Keycloak, Vault, an S3 file store, Temporal, Kafka, and monitoring with Prometheus and Grafana.

12
00:01:08,119 --> 00:01:14,369
Part zero brings up Postgres and Redis with a single command, and the rest arrives when a problem needs it.

13
00:01:14,369 --> 00:01:23,476
Part one builds the merchants: sign-up, email codes, company documents, two-admin approval, and logins and API clients in Keycloak.

14
00:01:23,476 --> 00:01:28,857
Part two adds the money rules: the commission, and the terminals we debit through.

15
00:01:28,857 --> 00:01:38,380
Part three handles cards: the Uzcard and Humo adapters as their own services, the BIN module with its bank directory, and a vault that stores only tokens.

16
00:01:38,380 --> 00:01:45,238
Part four builds the payment last, with monitoring, retries, and Kafka events, because it needs everything else.

17
00:01:45,238 --> 00:01:53,154
Part five deploys everything to Kubernetes, then moves the card vault into its own service, to show why clean boundaries matter.

18
00:01:53,154 --> 00:01:57,583
Next, we set up the project and bring the whole world up with one command.

